00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Thanks for joining us. This is Omni View, episode 128, Balance News Perspectives. Today is July 30th, 2026. Today's story is from around the world, multiple viewpoints, your call. American public opposition to the Iran conflict is now clear in fresh polling, narrowing the room for sustained US military action. A new AP ANOIC poll shows that most Americans believe the war in Iran has not been worth fighting.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:42.980
The results also indicate that public goals for the conflict differ from those stated by President Trump. The survey captures views across party lines at a moment when US involvement continues.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:51.240
Respondents were asked directly about the value of the military effort and about the specific objectives they consider most important.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:01:08.480
The poll was conducted by the Associated Press in partnership with Norark at the University of Chicago. The poll comes from a joint project of the Associated Press and Norark at the University of Chicago, which has tracked opinion on foreign engagements for years.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:19.080
Administration supporters argue that the poll reflects short-term fatigue rather than a full assessment of strategic aims such as preventing Iranian nuclear advances.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:32.480
Critics of continued involvement, including some members of Congress from both parties, point to the same numbers as evidence that the costs and lives and resources have already exceeded any achievable gains.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:48.040
The factual baseline is undisputed. The survey sampled US adults and found majority opposition to ongoing operations. The split centers on whether that opposition should alter operational decisions or simply register public sentiment.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:54.840
White House statements have continued to emphasize the importance of the mission even as the poll numbers circulated.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:09.420
One position holds that presidents retain constitutional authority to direct military operations even when polls show public doubt because elected leaders must weigh long-term security interests that surveys cannot fully capture.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.600
Supporters of this view note that past presidents continued engagements after similar readings.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:32.100
The competing position maintains that sustained public opposition eventually constrains funding and troop commitments through congressional action and that ignoring clear polling risks repeating earlier episodes in which domestic support eroded mid-conflict.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:39.060
Each side concedes that polls measure sentiment at one moment and do not dictate every tactical choice.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:46.160
What happens next? Congressional committees begin work on the next defense appropriations bill in the coming weeks.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:57.540
Shifting now to a strike reported in Kuwait. Kuwait's military reported that an Iranian strike struck a building belonging to a Chinese firm in northern Kuwait on Thursday morning.

